WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:04.640
We've seen the "Cinderella" story told a&nbsp;
dozen different ways, but we've never seen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:09.120
everyone's favorite underdog princess&nbsp;
like this. This Ella is a princess&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:14.240
made for 2021, and her movie has some&nbsp;
details that only adults will notice.

00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:19.440
Kids who aren't well-versed in music history&nbsp;
might think most of the music in "Cinderella"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:24.480
is original. However, the movie is actually&nbsp;
littered with many delightful takes on '70s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:30.160
'80s, and '90s music. The first song Cinderella&nbsp;
sings isn't original to the movie at all, in fact:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.160 --> 00:00:35.280
It's a take on the 1994 song "You&nbsp;
Gotta Be" by R&amp;B artist Des'ree.

00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:40.160
Younger audiences might recognize certain other&nbsp;
classic songs, like Prince Robert's version of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:45.120
Queen's "Somebody to Love," or Vivian's&nbsp;
rendition of Madonna's "Material Girl."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:49.440
Less omnipresently popular songs will&nbsp;
probably elude them, however -- but not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:52.800
the adults in the audience, who&nbsp;
will be jamming until the end.

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:56.400
The movie features appearances from&nbsp;
Salt-N-Pepa's "Whatta Man," Earth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.400 --> 00:01:01.040
Wind &amp; Fire's "Shining Star," as&nbsp;
well as Jennifer Lopez's 1999 hit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:05.360
"Let's Get Loud," which provides the&nbsp;
last, show-stopping musical number.

00:01:05.360 --> 00:01:10.400
Original songs like "Million to One" round out the&nbsp;
soundtrack, along with more recent additions to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:15.840
the world's pop songbook. Prince Robert and Ella's&nbsp;
charming slow dance to Ed Sheeran's "Perfect" is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:20.640
especially memorable. But otherwise, this movie&nbsp;
is a master class in recent music history.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:25.840
If kids haven't heard these jams before watching,&nbsp;
they'll certainly be blasting them on repeat now.

00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:32.320
"Cinderella" has a number of fairly unsubtle&nbsp;
innuendoes (we're looking at you, mice), but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.320 --> 00:01:37.840
Princess Laura's is especially attention-grabbing.&nbsp;
We'd expect nothing less from the posh-yet-savage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.840 --> 00:01:42.960
princess: We only get to know her for a few&nbsp;
minutes, but she completely steals the show.

00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:46.880
In her dismal meeting with Prince&nbsp;
Robert, during which she condescendingly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.880 --> 00:01:51.920
explains to him what a map is, she tries&nbsp;
to arrange a mutually beneficial marriage.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.920 --> 00:01:56.160
She leaves frustrated when Robert shows no&nbsp;
interest in her proposal, even though she's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.160 --> 00:02:00.560
perfectly chill with him hanging out with his&nbsp;
bros and never spending time together -- except&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.560 --> 00:02:05.040
for when they have to engage in what she&nbsp;
calls the disgusting practice of having a son.

00:02:05.040 --> 00:02:08.080
When she leaves the meeting in a&nbsp;
huff, she gets the last laugh when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.080 --> 00:02:11.120
she addresses the other princess&nbsp;
hopefuls on her way out the door.

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:17.760
It'll fly over children's heads, but Laura's dig&nbsp;
criticizes his desirability from top to bottom,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:22.800
from his brain down to his — well, you know.&nbsp;
Forget about marriage — Laura's parting dig&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:27.600
calls into question whether or not this dense&nbsp;
prince would be worth a romp in the hay at all.

00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:30.880
Here's a sentiment every&nbsp;
woman is tired of hearing:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.880 --> 00:02:33.280
"You'd be prettier if you did x, y, or z!"

00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:37.760
Countless women throughout history have&nbsp;
dealt with this condescending "compliment."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.760 --> 00:02:40.720
While young girls certainly face&nbsp;
their fair share of criticism,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:44.720
this particular bit of nastiness is&nbsp;
most often applied to adult women.

00:02:44.720 --> 00:02:49.440
Women who tear other women down out of&nbsp;
internalized misogyny also use this line,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:51.680
something "Cinderella" vividly captures:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.680 --> 00:02:55.120
Ella's stepmother Vivian constantly&nbsp;
does this to her stepdaughter.

00:02:55.120 --> 00:02:57.680
Never mind that Vivian forces her stepdaughter to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.680 --> 00:03:00.800
live in a dingy basement with&nbsp;
only a few mice for friends.

00:03:00.800 --> 00:03:04.960
The misogynistic undertones of most&nbsp;
fairy tale-based movies are prominent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.960 --> 00:03:09.760
and it's refreshing to see a modern take turn&nbsp;
these tropes on their head. This creates a more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.760 --> 00:03:14.080
positive message and gives young women the&nbsp;
tools they need to call out this behavior.

00:03:14.080 --> 00:03:18.320
Unlike most iterations of "Cinderella," this&nbsp;
story's Ella doesn't need to change herself to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.320 --> 00:03:23.280
win Prince Robert's favor: She catches his eye&nbsp;
in a ragged dress and messy hair. Even when Ella&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.280 --> 00:03:27.600
prepares for the ball, she doesn't get made over&nbsp;
into a glamorous stranger. She looks the same,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.600 --> 00:03:31.840
in fact, except she's wearing the dress&nbsp;
she designed. It's an empowering choice.

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:36.000
Not everyone can be as subtle&nbsp;
about innuendo as Princess Laura,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:41.280
but Ella's three best friends (yes, they're&nbsp;
mice) try their hardest. When Fab G turns&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:46.560
Ella's mice besties into three human footmen,&nbsp;
Ella is confused to discover they're male.

00:03:46.560 --> 00:03:48.880
Well, that's not how that works, Ella.

00:03:48.880 --> 00:03:52.480
James Corden's mouse-human asks&nbsp;
the question on everyone's mind:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.480 --> 00:03:56.640
"How do you think rats have more rats?"&nbsp;
Fab G is quick to steer the conversation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.640 --> 00:04:00.640
away from breaking the news to Ella that&nbsp;
mouse babies don't get delivered by storks.

00:04:00.640 --> 00:04:04.800
Things get even more uncomfortable after&nbsp;
the mice drop off Ella at the palace.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:08.320
There, James Corden's mouse-human&nbsp;
makes it weird again by announcing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.320 --> 00:04:11.360
that he's relieved himself&nbsp;
from his, um, front tail.

00:04:12.080 --> 00:04:16.880
Most kids watching "Cinderella" likely&nbsp;
recognize Idina Menzel and Camila Cabello:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.880 --> 00:04:21.600
Menzel voices Elsa of "Frozen" fame,&nbsp;
and Cabello is a famous pop star.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.600 --> 00:04:26.240
But kids tuning into this might not realize&nbsp;
that Billy Porter is Broadway royalty.

00:04:26.240 --> 00:04:31.440
Porter hit the scene in the early '90s when he&nbsp;
played Teen Angel in the 1994 "Grease" revival.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.440 --> 00:04:35.440
More roles soon followed, culminating&nbsp;
in the one that made Porter a household&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.440 --> 00:04:40.800
name in the world of theater: Lola in 2013's&nbsp;
"Kinky Boots." Porter originated this role,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.800 --> 00:04:46.240
and garnered multiple awards, including the Tony&nbsp;
for Best Actor in a Musical, in the process.

00:04:46.240 --> 00:04:51.600
While certain teens might recognize Porter from&nbsp;
"American Horror Story" or "Pose," even they might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.600 --> 00:04:56.080
be ignorant of his luminous Broadway history.&nbsp;
After watching "Cinderella," however, they might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.080 --> 00:05:00.960
be inspired to check it out, as Porter is the&nbsp;
perfect person to take on the role of Fab G.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.960 --> 00:05:06.480
He always brings a unique pizazz to any part he&nbsp;
takes on -- especially ones as glamorous as this.

00:05:07.360 --> 00:05:11.440
Most kids would rather enjoy the movie&nbsp;
than focus on its glaring plot holes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.440 --> 00:05:14.640
As it turns out, Fab G agrees with that sentiment.

00:05:14.640 --> 00:05:18.240
That said, adults can't help picking&nbsp;
apart plotlines that don't make sense,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.240 --> 00:05:22.560
even when magic is involved. For example,&nbsp;
Fab makes it abundantly clear that when the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.560 --> 00:05:26.800
clock strikes midnight, the magic will wear&nbsp;
off. But despite the fact that magic does&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.800 --> 00:05:32.800
eventually flicker out of every single thing Fab G&nbsp;
bewitched, the glass slippers remain intact. Huh?

00:05:32.800 --> 00:05:36.800
We watch as Ella's dress flutters away into&nbsp;
the wind, the human mice turn back into their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.800 --> 00:05:42.560
four-legged selves, and the carriage transforms&nbsp;
into a hunk of junk. Yet days after the ball, Ella&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.560 --> 00:05:47.760
still has a fully intact glass slipper hiding in&nbsp;
her basket, while Prince Robert has the other one.

00:05:47.760 --> 00:05:53.040
That's not the only odd thing adults will notice,&nbsp;
either. Is Ella's shoe size really that special&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.040 --> 00:05:57.680
that no one else matches it? Moreover, the&nbsp;
prince has seen Ella both dolled up and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.680 --> 00:06:02.160
in tattered clothes. If the shoe fits an entirely&nbsp;
different woman, is he really ready to abandon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.160 --> 00:06:07.520
everything he knows about Ella and run off with&nbsp;
a rando? Is he completely face-blind? He doesn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.520 --> 00:06:12.480
even find her with the shoe, so this fairly&nbsp;
nonsensical plotline isn't even necessary.

00:06:13.280 --> 00:06:17.440
Throughout "Cinderella," the king seems&nbsp;
to be overcompensating for something.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.440 --> 00:06:21.920
His frequent power trips aren't exactly out&nbsp;
of character for a king, but his particular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.920 --> 00:06:26.720
preoccupations imply he might be insecure about&nbsp;
something only adults will take notice of.

00:06:26.720 --> 00:06:30.320
King Rowan constantly talks about&nbsp;
the size of everything around him.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.320 --> 00:06:35.280
Characters bring up the fact that he's had his&nbsp;
minions make his throne slightly larger not once,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.280 --> 00:06:39.040
but twice. This bit of innuendo gets&nbsp;
a laugh out of the older audience,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.040 --> 00:06:43.280
to be sure. But it also develops into a&nbsp;
lesson everyone can learn from. We all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.280 --> 00:06:48.080
have things we're insecure about, regardless of&nbsp;
our social status, fame, or fortune. But how we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.080 --> 00:06:53.120
treat the people around us is more important&nbsp;
than whatever trivial detail we agonize over.

00:06:53.120 --> 00:06:57.360
Despite the king's self-consciousness, he&nbsp;
learns to stop putting down his loved ones&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.360 --> 00:06:59.680
and to respect and honor each of their strengths,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.680 --> 00:07:02.960
regardless of the norms of the&nbsp;
time. Talk about character growth.

00:07:03.680 --> 00:07:08.320
Whirlwind romances are the norm in princess&nbsp;
films, but in reality, it takes time to get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.320 --> 00:07:12.960
to know someone. Of course, in the eras that&nbsp;
inspired the setting of "Cinderella," modern&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.960 --> 00:07:18.960
dating didn't exist. But an adult still can't help&nbsp;
but flinch at Ella and Prince Robert's choices.

00:07:18.960 --> 00:07:21.520
Proposing marriage after one date is absurd,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.520 --> 00:07:25.040
and the prince is selfish to ask&nbsp;
her to give up her dreams. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.040 --> 00:07:29.760
the tables eventually turn when Prince Robert&nbsp;
gives up his dreams for Ella's sake. But while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.760 --> 00:07:34.320
Robert may say that he's not very interested&nbsp;
in royal life or becoming king, he's making&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.320 --> 00:07:39.520
a very rash decision at a young age that will&nbsp;
affect his life and the future of the kingdom.

00:07:39.520 --> 00:07:43.200
Robert does, at one point, even tell his&nbsp;
father that there's nothing he wants more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.200 --> 00:07:46.560
than to be king. He needs to give&nbsp;
himself a chance to mature into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.560 --> 00:07:50.640
wanting that role before running off&nbsp;
with Ella, lest he come to resent her.

00:07:50.640 --> 00:07:55.520
There's no denying that Gwen is a better fit for&nbsp;
the throne, and Ella and Robert don't get married&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.520 --> 00:08:00.000
immediately. But this is still a case of someone&nbsp;
-- a very young someone -- giving up everything&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:04.640
for another person. It's the prince instead of the&nbsp;
princess this time, but it still demonstrates a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.640 --> 00:08:09.280
lack of foresight the grown-ups in the audience&nbsp;
will recognize as potentially destructive.

